      "criteria": [
        {
          "criterion": "Every question in quiz.json tests a point about the target of the change that the case brief states",
          "met": true,
          "reason": "Every question targets the brief's stated change: verdict from files alone (q1, q4, q6), the invariant vs behavior split (q2, q3, q9), runs as result directories (q7, q10), scenarios as validated data files (q8), and CLI exit codes (q10); the scrub question (q5) covers the runner's design for leaving files a keyless grader can read."
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Every question sits at an altitude that the case brief allows, as the brief describes that altitude",
          "met": true,
          "reason": "All questions ask why the shape was chosen or what its rules are (skip semantics, leak marks, all-or-nothing writes, pre-session validation, the extension path), staying above function names and flags, which is the design altitude the brief allows."
        },
        {
          "criterion": "No question asks about an incidental part of the change that the case brief lists",
          "met": true,
          "reason": "No question touches the brief's incidentals: the test/scenarios plugin fixtures, package.json script names, or the boundary test edit; those appear only in the plan's incidental metadata."
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Every wrong option of a single or multi question is a plausible misreading of the change, not filler",
          "met": true,
          "reason": "Every wrong option states a coherent misreading a reader could hold, such as grading as a consistency check or memory saving, the split as code location or run mode, a stop as a model failure, cost as a judged expectation, or a leak hiding as a normal redaction; none is filler."
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Each question's key_points state what a correct answer must cover, at the same altitude as the question",
          "met": true,
          "reason": "Each question's key points restate the design rule and its reason a full answer must cover at the question's own level, e.g. q2's rates-and-cost rule, q3's skip-is-excluded-from-rate rule, q7's rename-last atomicity."
        },
        {
          "criterion": "The questions go outside in: the first ones cover what the change is for and its main boundary, narrower mechanisms and details come later, and no question is more central to the change than one asked before it, except the last",
          "met": true,
          "reason": "The order runs outside in: the purpose (q1) and the main invariant/behavior split (q2) come first, then skip and leak rules, file and scrub mechanics, crash and validation details, then the extension path, with the synthesis last; no later question is clearly more central than an earlier one."
        },
        {
          "criterion": "When quiz.json has two or more questions, the last one applies several points of the change to one new case the change does not spell out",
          "met": true,
          "reason": "q10 applies several points to one fresh case the change does not spell out: a last-prompt timeout combined with an earlier invariant failure, testing exit-code precedence, directory persistence and checking, the incomplete report, and skip-vs-failure semantics."
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Every question can be answered by a person who understands what the change does and why, with the code closed: no question depends on recalling a function name, a file name, a flag, an exit code, or an exact value, unless that name or value is itself the point the author must own",
          "met": true,
          "reason": "Answers follow from understanding the design with the code closed; aigent and multeval appear only as context, and the one value recall, exit 1, is part of the exit-code contract the target itself names as the point."
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Every prompt, option, and key point is plain English: short sentences, common words, one idea each, with no jargon the question does not need and no filler or marketing words",
          "met": true,
          "reason": "Prompts, options, and key points are short plain sentences, one idea each, with domain terms (transcript, scrub, enable) only where the question needs them and no filler or marketing words."
        }
      ]
    }
